'''Sare''' ({{lang|bur|Sare}}, meaning "Hyper" in [[Burawi language|Burawi]]) is a [[Burawa|Burawi]] {{W|multinational corporation|multinational}} technology company headquartered in [[Mazar]], [[Burawa]]. Founded in 2008 by [[Warsame Dheere]] and [[Cabdirashiid Koofur]], the company operates a {{W|platform economy|platform}} offering {{W|food delivery}}, {{W|ride-hailing}}, {{W|bike sharing}}, and {{W|peer-to-peer marketplace}} services across [[Southern Kaftia]].

Sare began as a food delivery startup in Mazar before expanding into peer-to-peer delivery and ride-hailing. The company grew rapidly following {{W|venture capital}} funding from [[Litmus Ventures]] in 2010 and became the largest ride-hailing platform in Southern Kaftia after [[Hop]] exited the region in 2014. The company held its {{W|initial public offering}} on the {{W|Burawa Stock Exchange|BSX}} in 2015.

As of 2025, Sare serves approximately 50 million users across Southern Kaftia and maintains a market capitalization of approximately $12 billion. The company has faced controversy over worker classification and immigration practices in Burawa and other Southern Kaftian markets.

== History ==

=== Founding (2008) ===
Sare was founded in 2008 in [[Mazar]], [[Burawa]], by [[Warsame Dheere]] and [[Cabdirashiid Koofur]]. The company initially operated as a food delivery service, connecting restaurants with customers in Mazar. The founding was inspired by the emergence of food delivery applications in other countries, though Burawa's regulatory environment required adaptations to the business model.

The company's launch coincided with the Burawa government's introduction of the [[Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)]]. Originally designed to address labor shortages in the publicly-owned [[oil industry of Burawa|oil industry]], the program allowed companies to hire immigrant workers for temporary positions without committing to the standard 10-year work-to-citizenship pathway. The Act proved essential to Sare's business model, enabling the company to rapidly scale its workforce by hiring workers from abroad—a necessity in a labor market where Burawi citizens rarely seek gig economy employment due to the accessibility of public sector jobs.

=== Early growth (2009–2011) ===
In 2009, Sare expanded its delivery service to include peer-to-peer transactions, allowing individuals to send items to one another rather than only restaurant-to-consumer deliveries. This expansion established the foundation for the company's later [[Sare Suuq|marketplace platform]].

The company received significant {{W|venture capital}} funding from [[Litmus Ventures]] in 2010, enabling rapid expansion across Burawa. By 2011, Sare operated in most major Burawi cities.

=== Ride-hailing expansion (2012–2014) ===
In 2012, Sare launched its ride-hailing service, '''Sare Socdo''', entering competition with [[Hop]], a global rideshare company from the [[United Midlands]], and [[Z2Z]], a local Burawi startup. The {{W|ride-hailing}} market in Southern Kaftia was highly competitive, with Hop holding significant market share.

In 2014, Hop sold its Southern Kaftian division and exited the region, citing regulatory challenges and market conditions. Sare acquired a significant portion of Hop's former market share, becoming the largest ride-hailing platform in Southern Kaftia.

=== IPO and expansion (2015–present) ===
Sare held its {{W|initial public offering}} on the [[Mazar Stock Exchange]] in 2015. The company used the capital raised to expand operations across Southern Kaftia, launching services in [[Galwa]], [[Fayeha]], [[Dakare]], [[Maianka]], [[Orma]], [[Jarin]], and other Southern Kaftian nations.

In 2022, the Burawa government investigated Sare as part of a commission considering whether peer-to-peer marketplaces should be classified as [[Essential companies in Burawa|essential services]]. The investigation was subsequently dropped without action.

== Services ==

=== Sare Cunto ===
{{main|Sare Cunto}}
'''Sare Cunto''' ({{lang|bur|Cunto}}, "food") is the company's original food delivery service, launched in 2008. The platform connects restaurants with customers, offering delivery through a network of drivers. Sare Cunto remains one of the largest food delivery platforms in Burawa.

=== Sare Socdo ===
{{main|Sare Socdo}}
'''Sare Socdo''' ({{lang|bur|Socdo}}, "go") is the company's ride-hailing service, launched in 2012. Operating across Southern Kaftia, the platform connects passengers with drivers for on-demand transportation. Following Hop's exit from Southern Kaftia in 2014, Sare Socdo became the region's largest ride-hailing platform.

=== Sare Baaskiil ===
{{main|Sare Baaskiil}}
'''Sare Baaskiil''' ({{lang|bur|Baaskiil}}, "bicycle") is the company's docked bike-sharing service, operating exclusively in [[Mazar]]. The service provides publicly-accessible bicycles at designated stations throughout the city.

=== Sare Suuq ===
{{main|Sare Suuq}}
'''Sare Suuq''' ({{lang|bur|Suuq}}, "market") is the company's peer-to-peer marketplace platform, launched in 2009. The service allows users to list items for sale and arrange delivery through Sare's network. Sare Suuq has been described as analogous to {{W|Facebook Marketplace}} combined with delivery services.

== Geographic coverage ==

Sare operates across [[Southern Kaftia]], encompassing both [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and [[Southeastern Kaftia]]. The company maintains operations in:

* [[Burawa]] (primary market)
* [[Galwa]]
* [[Fayeha]]
* [[Kijura]]
* [[Dakare]]
* [[Maianka]]
* [[Orma]]
* [[Jarin]]
* [[Itegar]]
* [[Triphylia]]

The company's services are available in most major cities across these nations, with the exception of '''Sare Baaskiil''', which operates only in Mazar.

== Controversies ==

=== Worker classification and immigration practices ===

==== Burawa ====
In Burawa, [[Labour law in Burawa|labour law]] requires that drivers for ride-hailing and delivery platforms be classified as full-time employees rather than independent contractors. Sare has faced criticism and government scrutiny over its labor practices, particularly regarding its use of the [[Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)|Temporary Worker Program]].

The company was accused of hiring predominantly immigrant labor at low wages and engaging in rapid worker churn—hiring workers for approximately one year before terminating their employment. Critics argued that this practice exploited the Temporary Worker Program, which was designed to provide a pathway to stable employment and citizenship.

Burawi citizens rarely seek employment in gig economy jobs due to the relative ease of obtaining public sector employment. This labor market dynamic has contributed to Sare's reliance on immigrant workers.

The Burawa government investigated Sare for alleged [[immigration fraud]] related to these practices. The investigation examined whether the company had violated the terms of the Temporary Worker Program.

==== Other Southern Kaftian markets ====
Outside Burawa, Sare has faced worker classification controversies similar to those encountered by other global ride-hailing platforms. In [[Galwa]], the company faced legal challenges over the classification of drivers as independent contractors rather than employees. Similar issues arose in [[Dakare]] and [[Maianka]].

=== 2022 essential service investigation ===
In 2022, the Burawa government established a commission to investigate whether peer-to-peer marketplaces should be classified as [[Essential companies in Burawa|essential services]], which would subject them to additional regulatory oversight. Sare was investigated as part of this commission's work. The investigation was ultimately dropped without regulatory action.

